data.content:
You're right — in MSTest, `Assert.AreEqual` takes `(expected, actual)`, but your arguments are reversed (`actual, expected`). Here's the fix:

```csharp
// Before (incorrect order)
Assert.AreEqual(result, 5);
Assert.AreEqual(result, 0);

// After (correct order: expected, actual)
Assert.AreEqual(5, result);
Assert.AreEqual(0, result);
```

This matters because when a test fails, MSTest uses the argument order to generate the error message (e.g., "Expected: 5, Actual: 7"). Swapped arguments produce confusing failure messages.
